I can tell you what the situation is regarding my own Ph.D. materials.  When
I published my dissertation, I retained the copyright for everything in it
including data and text.  In the event that more than some threshold number
of copies of those volumes are sold, I am entitled to receive royalties on the
proceeds.  Presumably that means I still own the materials I generated while
I was writing the thing.  While I freely permit my advisor and anyone else
in the department who is interested to use my data, so long as they include
a reference to the source (as I would anyone who had a copy of the completed
work), when I leave the school I will take my electronic files with me just
as I would the paper ones.  As a minimum of civilized behavior, I would remove
my electronic files from the computer system so as not to occupy space to
which I am no longer entitled.  My advisor has a complete bound copy of my
dissertation, and so would not be losing access to anything I have done.  Of
course, I own the copyright on his volumes, as I indicated previously.

All of the above is strictly my own opinion, unless otherwise noted.  No-one
else should be held responsible for what I think.

