In article <3kjpq9$3st@martha.utk.edu> dbd@panacea.phys.utk.edu (David DeLaney) writes:
>vapspcx@cad.gatech.edu (S. Keith Graham) writes:
> ... copyright law is *very*
>clear on this point: a work belongs solely to its creator, and not to
>any person or institution that the creator may happen to be ...
>working for, *unless* said creator has previously signed a contract granting
>rights to the other entity.

While I don't believe that anything created by a student using school
facilities belongs to the school unless the student is a true employee
of the school and the creation is part of her work, the statement above
is nonsense.

From Section 101 of the Copyright Act:
  A "work made for hire" is --
  (1) a work prepared by an employee within the scope of his or her
      emplyement; ...

From Section 201 of the Copyright Act:
  In the case of a work made for hire, the employer or other person for
  whom the work was prepared is considered the author for purposes of
  this title, and, unless the parties have expressly agreed otherwise in
  a written instrument signed by them, owns all of the rights comprised
  in the copyright.

So no signed contract is necessary for an employer to own the copyright in
(and to be the author of) a work made for hire.  There is the need for a
signed contract for the employee to own the rights.  So the real question
is whether something is a work made for hire.

				Lee Hollaar

