>>
>Fellowship/Stipend ammounts in excess of tuition and fees are considered 
>taxable income by the IRS.

At least around here, all work done to fullfill any degree requirement
is not a "work for hire". That is, if you are a research assistant
it's not work for hire. If you are a teaching assistant and there
is a teaching requirement you have not yet finished meeting, it's not
a work for hire.  If you are a T.A. and HAVE already finished the required
T.A. time you could indeed be ask to produce some sort of written
"work for hire" but around here we don't consider it that. In other
words, if your job is as described above, the copyright of any work you create
is owned by you. You PhD thesis is copyright by you. Sometimes this
may actually matter ... mine was reviewed favorably by Nature magazine,
for example, and a few copies have been sold :-) :-).

If, on the other hand, you are, for example, an undergrad hired to
write a computer program, that may very well be work for hire.

