Regarding the issue of whether financial assistance in the form of 
scholarships, fellowships, and other "non-service" awards creates any 
sort of employment relationship between the grantor and the receiver, the 
answer is "no." Work produced by a student using such funds 
could not be claimed by the grantor unless a specific agreement was 
signed between the parties. I've received fellowships and scholarships of 
all sorts over the past eight years and never signed any sort of agreement.

If you are employed at a wage to do a particular task or job, then any 
work you produce in the course of your employment is the property of your 
employer. In the course of your employment means on employer-time using 
employer resources. Some employers have interpreted this to mean that if 
you use their resources on your own time, they still own the work; some 
have been successful in court and some have not. Alot boils down to what 
those resources are: if you use your office on the weekends as a quiet 
place to write a book using your own paper, then the courts are unlikely to 
uphold an employers claim. If you use all the resources and materials of a 
large chemistry lab after hours, then the employer may be granted a share of 
the rights to your work.

There have been several cases of students sued by Universities for 
failing to turn over patents developed as part of paid service. One chap 
was even jailed about a year ago. I haven't researched this aspect of 
copyright, though, so I don't know if there are similar instances. The 
patent cases were a bit complex because the service work also formed part 
of the student's graduate study. It's a common practice for students to 
try to kill two birds with one stone by combining paid work with 
dissertation research. This is evidently a grey area of copyright and 
patent in part because traditionally the professional ethics of higher 
education took care of relations between students and their employing 
professor-researchers. In recent years, external grant funding conditions 
and the increasing desire for lucrative patents and copyrights by 
financially-strapped universities has begun to undermine those ethics. 
Professor-researchers no longer have as much discretion to cut some slack 
to their students by not enforcing their employer rights.

I'd guess that system administrators have the option of requiring a 
professor who is asking for data files either to produce something from 
the file owner which grants rights, or to produce some sort of reasonable 
evidence that the files were produced as part of service. Of course, a 
reasonably wise employer would make sure that files were stored in 
his/her disk space with rights granted to the employee!


