sears@uh.edu (Paul S. Sears) writes:
>We are facing a problem that we do not think has been adequately  
>addressed.  First, the overall question is:

>Who owns a student user's work (project, etc - not email)
>   a) when the student is enrolled at a public university.
>   b) when a student leaves a public univerity.

>We already understand that email is protected by the ECPA, but we are  
>mainly interested in graduate/phd research work.  When a student leaves,  
>who retains the rights to the research completed by the student - his/her  
>advisor or the student?  

     Absent any employment relationship, clearly the student owns
the rights to anything they did.  The student is a customer of the
institution.  Employment may complicate the issue, but where there's
no employment relationship, the situation seems clear.

     It's worth noting that this applies regardless of any "university
policy" or "grant restrictions".  If you're not a party to the agreement,
it doesn't bind you.  It's quite possible for a university to get in a
position where a student owns some intellectual property right that the
university is contractually committed to deliver to a funding agency.

     I would absolutely not sign away any intellectual property rights
as a student, if there was any possibility of commercial development.
I've always been extremely careful about this, and it's done very well
for me financially.

     It helps to file your own copyrights and patents in your own name,
after which you can argue about ownership from a position of strength.

					John Nagle

