Suggestions in a newsgroup are going to be general, but such is the 
medium. <g>. The area you should be looking at is the power and authority 
of the University over the activities of its students. It has been a long 
time since I looked at those issues, but my recollection is that some of 
the arguments that applied to high school students may apply to college 
students.

There is also the issue of whose computer you are using, when the notice 
first appeared, and the exact text of the notice, if that was not a 
direct quote that you posted.

As to the quoted comment from the University, "people" certainly do have 
a right to privacy in their email, so says the ECPA. Whether college 
students using a university computer are "people" in that context is 
another question, and that is where your inquiry should start, IMHO.

There are a number of cases which provide for the NON privacy of 
telephone conversations in a business setting, and there are a few cases 
around the country dealing with email and the business setting. I don't 
have citations handy, but that is another area for you to pursue.

My recollection is however, that businesses CAN monitor and intercept the 
telephone calls of their employees, with proper disclosure. You may find 
that the same is true of universities and their email systems.


