The *school* by itself cannot enforce any such thing, unless perhaps the
novel in question was written entirely on university time *and* on
university property. The school may *claim* what it wishes, and some
people may choose to go along with them, but copyright law is *very*
clear on this point: a work belongs solely to its creator, and not to
any person or institution that the creator may happen to be living with or
working for, *unless* said creator has previously signed a contract granting
rights to the other entity. If Georgia Tech is forcing its employess to sign
such a contract as a condition of employment, my gut feeling is that Georgia
Tech is setting itself up for one *hell* of a class-action lawsuit sometime
in the future...

>So any work created by a student is going to be the property of
>the school.  (For example, this posting's copyright is probably
>jointly owned by the school.)

Nope. The quoted posting's copyright is fully and solely owned by you, Keith
Graham, unless you have explicitly signed something which assigns future
copyright to electronic written works you create to someone else.

