cburian@ux4.cso.uiuc.edu (Christopher J Burian) writes:

>As far as I'm concerned, all this voting crap is a bunch of baloney.
>Somebody ought to be able start up a newsgroup called k-12.chicken- 
>hawks.seeking.pre-pubescent.homo.boys.and.girls if he wants without 
>interference from dumbass netcops.

He can. The method is simple: buy a suitable computer; install news
software on it; create the group on that machine. He now has a
functional group. If he wants it to be created on other machines as
well, he should issue a newgroup control message for it.

But there is no guarantee that the owners and/or administrators of
those other machines will do anything about this control message - in
fact, with that name, it is extremely likely that most won't. The
crucial thing if you want a worldwide newsgroup (or even a regional
one) is to persuade other owners/adminstrators in large numbers to
create the group on their machines.

This "voting crap", as you put it, is simply a method to persuade them
that has been established to work in practice. Your hypothetical
person (or anyone else) is free to try any other method to do so: for
instance, he could pick up the phone and start calling them up. They,
in turn, are free to ignore him - and are very likely to do so unless
he comes up with a method which they believe has significant
advantages for them over the established methods. (NB "they believe",
not "he believes", and "advantages for them", not "advantages for him"
or "advantages for the readers of the proposed group".)

In short, if you only want to create groups on your own machines, you
can: it's as simple and straightforward as that. (Case in point: many
sites have groups for internal discussions which have never gone
through any sort of voting process.) But if you want to create groups
*on other people's machines*, you have to use methods that are
acceptable to them.

