rankin@mscf.uky.edu (David W. Rankin Jr.) writes:
>*warning: I am not a lawyer. Do not interpret this post as legal advice.*

Don't worry, I'm not either. (Though if I had become one, I might actually
have a job at the moment...)

>David DeLaney <dbd@panacea.phys.utk.edu> wrote:
>>The school may *claim* what it wishes, and some
>>people may choose to go along with them, but copyright law is *very*
>>clear on this point: a work belongs solely to its creator, and not to
>>any person or institution that the creator may happen to be living with or
>>working for, *unless* said creator has previously signed a contract granting
>>rights to the other entity. If Georgia Tech is forcing its employess to sign
>>such a contract as a condition of employment, my gut feeling is that Georgia
>>Tech is setting itself up for one *hell* of a class-action lawsuit sometime
>>in the future...
[...]
>Actually, if Mr. Graham was at work when posting the message, then a
>valid claim to ownership (at least in part) would probably be valid,
>since it is work on salary time. If the post was after "business hours",
>then the claims would be less valid, i.e. probably bogus.

The point of the original posting was that Georgia Tech is claiming this
not only for its salaried employees but for its *students* as well, including
graduate students; as far as I know, students, even those receiving fellowships
or grants-in-aid or teaching assistantships, are *not* considered to be
"employees" of a university. I can somewhat see the applicability to a faculty
member who used school property and resources while on the job to write a
book; I do *not* see the applicability to either the case of that faculty
member writing the book on weekends at home, or to the case of the faculty
member's teaching assistant writing a book whilst the assistantship is
helping to pay for his living expenses while the assistant is attending
graduate-level classes...

[Apparently what I said, quoted above, isn't *quite* true, and actual salaried
employees can have their work-on-company-time claimed by the company, and
the company can *try*, with varying degrees of success, to claim ownership
of stuff created by the employee while "off the clock"... but I still feel
that any company that does in fact take this stance is putting itself in for
more trouble than it's worth...]

