  "The Odyssey" and, perhaps, "Oedipus Rex".  That's it. At the end
  of the year, students would have have been intruduced to some
  fundamental works around which the West was constituted; more
  importantly, they would have learned to read carefully and to
  think for themselves.""Yale students are asked to read too much".
     Now this is of interest to me, since Western Civilization is 
  "constituted" around Christ, who appeared when the Classical
  Culture was in its terminal stage.  In fact, if we want to relate 
  to a past Civilization, a better model would be the Egyptian
  Culture with which we share a common belief in the infinite
  extension of Time and we share the Cross, the Symbol of Ever-
  lasting Life.  As to the literature to be included in my idea of
  a proper course in Western Civilization?  Plenty of it, gathering 
  dust in the Yale library.  PUBLIUS at <alt.fan.publius>  	

